1. Problem Breakdown (Simple Summary)
Goal: Build one clean, unique list of every API endpoint across two separate tables (api_calls and rate_limits).
Cleaning Rule 1 (Trailing slashes): Paths like /api/v1/orders/ and /api/v1/orders are the same endpoint—strip any trailing / at the end.
Cleaning Rule 2 (Group auth sub-routes): Any path starting with /api/v1/auth (like /api/v1/auth/login or /api/v1/auth/logout) should be rolled up into just /api/v1/auth.
Deduplication Rule: Each cleaned endpoint should appear only once in the final output.

2. The Intuition (How to Think About It)
Imagine you have two messy lists of website URLs written by different teams—one from server logs (api_calls) and one from security settings (rate_limits). Before you can combine them into one master list, you need to standardize how the URLs are written so duplicates actually match.
Stack the two lists together (UNION vs. UNION ALL):
We want endpoints that appear in either table. In SQL, stacking two tables vertically is done with UNION.
Interview Tip: UNION automatically removes duplicate rows, whereas UNION ALL keeps duplicates. (Note: On huge tables like 500M rows, it's best to clean the strings first or deduplicate per table before combining, or use UNION on the cleaned values so duplicates created after cleaning—like /api/v1/auth/login and /api/v1/auth/token both becoming /api/v1/auth—also get merged into one row!)
Strip trailing slashes (RTRIM or TRIM):
RTRIM(endpoint, '/') chops off any / at the far right end of the text. So /api/v1/orders/ becomes /api/v1/orders.
Collapse /api/v1/auth/... into /api/v1/auth (CASE WHEN + LIKE):
Check if the cleaned path starts with /api/v1/auth using LIKE '/api/v1/auth%' (the % wildcard means "followed by anything or nothing").
If it matches, replace the whole string with '/api/v1/auth'. Otherwise, keep the trimmed path as-is.
Deduplicate after cleaning:
If you apply UNION after cleaning both tables (or wrap a UNION ALL inside a SELECT DISTINCT), /api/v1/auth/login and /api/v1/auth/signup both turn into /api/v1/auth and automatically merge into a single row.

with endpoints as (
select endpoint from api_calls
union
select endpoint from rate_limits 
)
select distinct
  case when RTRIM(TRIM(endpoint),'/') like '/api/v1/auth%' then '/api/v1/auth'
       else RTRIM(TRIM(endpoint),'/') 
  end as endpoint
from endpoints
